INSTALL
=======

How to build Odin, install it and start playing. Every option and setting
is in OPTIONS.


BUILD
-----

Linux. You need GCC (C++20), CMake 3.20 or later, pkg-config, awk,
glslangValidator, the OpenGL and X11 development files and an OpenGL 4.3
driver. SDL 3, GLEW, Jansson and the Vulkan headers come from the system,
or from 3rdparty/ when it has none:

 $ git clone --recurse-submodules <repository> odin && cd odin
 $ cmake -B build
 $ cmake --build build -j

The program is build/bin/odin, its data build/share/odin.

Windows. Build with MSYS2 UCRT64:

 $ pacman -S mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-gcc mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-cmake \
       mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-ninja mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-glslang gawk git
 $ cmake -B build -G Ninja -DODIN_BUNDLED_LIBS=ON && cmake --build build

macOS. Install the Xcode Command Line Tools, CMake and the LunarG Vulkan
SDK (then: source ~/VulkanSDK/<version>/setup-env.sh), or use Homebrew:

 $ brew install cmake pkgconf sdl3 glew jansson vulkan-headers vulkan-loader \
       molten-vk glslang
 $ cmake -B build && cmake --build build -j
 $ cmake --install build --prefix stage
 $ MOLTENVK=$(brew --prefix molten-vk)/lib/libMoltenVK.dylib \
       dist/macos/make-app.sh stage Odin.app

Build options (cmake -B build -D<option>=<value>):

 ODIN_NATIVE=OFF         No -march=native (for packages).
 ODIN_LTO=OFF            No link-time optimization.
 ODIN_PGO=generate|use   Profile-guided build: 4 to 15 % faster.
 ODIN_GLES=ON            OpenGL ES 3.1 instead of OpenGL (Linux).
 ODIN_BUNDLED_LIBS=ON    Always use 3rdparty/ (OFF: never).
 ODIN_OPT_FLAGS=<flags>  Default "-O3 -fomit-frame-pointer
                         -fno-stack-protector".
 ODIN_VERSION=<text>     The version, when building without git.
 GLSLANG=<path>          The glslangValidator to use.

Leave CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE empty (Release turns the asserts off).

Profile-guided build: build with ODIN_PGO=generate, play a few games, then
build again with ODIN_PGO=use.


INSTALL
-------

 $ cmake -B build -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=$HOME/.local
 $ cmake --build build -j && cmake --install build

The default prefix is /usr/local. For a package:

 $ DESTDIR=<root> cmake --install build --prefix /usr


FIRST START
-----------

1. Put the games in one folder, as MAME keeps them: a zip, a 7z or a folder
   per game, named after its MAME set: airtrix, airtrixo, braveff,
   pharrier, sgnascar, sgnascaro, swracer, von4. Two shared sets are
   needed too, in the same folder (or in a BIOS folder): hikaru and mie.
   The Gaelco games (atvtrack, atvtracka, gfootbal, smashdrv, smashdrvb,
   smashdrvs) go in the same folder. They need no shared set, and keep
   their operator settings and scores in kept.bin in their saves folder.

2. Start Odin. Its library shows the games. Click "Games folder..." and
   choose your folder. A game with missing files says which files, and in
   which MAME set they are.

3. Choose a game with the arrows, a gamepad or the mouse, then press Enter
   (or click it again).

4. The first time you play a game, calibrate its controls first, in its
   test menu (TEST: key 0, or Guide held with R3), as on the cabinet:
   every game has a page for it (Air Trix: CALIBRATION, Brave
   Firefighters: NOZZLE SENSOR SETTING...). The game then reads your
   controls right.

From a terminal:

 $ odin -R <games folder> -r <game>

Pictures: the title screens of the library and the bezels (around the
game, page Display, Bezel) come in Odin-Artwork.zip, released with Odin.
Extract it beside the program: it makes the folder artwork/ there, which
Odin then uses (an installed Odin: extract it into Odin's folder, see
FILES). Pictures of your own go there under the same names.

Optional sets: jvs13551 (the program of the I/O board), and for NASCAR
18wheelr (its I/O board) and srally2 (its drive board, the motor of the
wheel). Without them Odin imitates those boards; the games play the same.


PLAYING
-------

Keyboard (all keys can be changed, page Hotkeys and page Input):

 Escape, F10   menu (the game pauses)
 1, 2          Start of player 1, 2
 5, 6          coins
 0, 9          TEST and SERVICE (the test menus), with any device
 P             pause
 F2, F4        save, load the state
 Ctrl+R        reset
 F11           full screen
 F6            Virtual-On card in or out
 F7            hide the cursor or show it
 Ctrl+C        close the game (back to the library)
 Ctrl+Q        quit

Gamepad: Guide opens the menu, Select puts in a coin. Guide held with
Start quits, with Back pauses, with RB saves, with LB loads, with B
closes the game. A gamepad without Guide (or whose Guide another program
takes) holds another button instead, set on the page Hotkeys; the menu is
then that button with A.

Moving the mouse over the window shows a bar at its top: it sets the same
things as the menu, without pausing the game (not while the cursor is
hidden, as when the mouse aims in Brave Firefighters: F7 shows it).


LIGHT GUNS
----------

In Brave Firefighters the mouse aims for player 1. A light gun (Sinden,
GUN4IR, XGunner, Aimtrak, GunCon...) or a second mouse can play for each
player: choose it on the page Input. On Linux your user must be in the
group input.

A Sinden needs its white border: page Input, options, Sinden border.

Light guns with recoil or a vibration motor can follow the game, as on
the cabinet (the nozzle of Brave Firefighters). Turn on page Outputs, Send
outputs: a program that reads the outputs of MAME (Hook Of The Reaper,
QMamehook, OutputHooker, the Sinden software) then drives the gun. Odin
can also drive some guns itself, without such a program: page Outputs,
Light guns lists them. On Linux your user must be in the group of the
gun's serial port (uucp or dialout).


WHEELS
------

NASCAR, ATV Track and Smashing Drive steer with a wheel: one player per
cabinet, so one wheel per Odin (link cabinets to race each other). Choose
the wheel on the page Input. A known wheel (Logitech G25 to G923,
Thrustmaster T150 or T300, MOZA R12 and others; only the G29 was tried)
comes with its pedals, paddles, Start and Select set. On another, only the
steering: click a control there, then move the pedal or press the button
you want for it. A wheel with force feedback turns as the game asks. Set
how far it turns (Wheel rotation: Full, its whole turn, unless you choose
an angle) and how hard (Wheel force) in its options on the page Input. On
Linux your user must be in the group input for the force feedback.

Other joysticks (a flight stick for Planet Harriers) are set the same way.


LINKED CABINETS
---------------

Linked cabinets: Planet Harriers 2, Star Wars Racer and Virtual-On 4,
NASCAR 8. Each cabinet is one Odin, on one computer or several of a local
network (by cable). On the page Link of each Odin: the network board on, a
cabinet number (1 is the master), and the address of the next cabinet;
the last one points to the first. Two Odins on one computer each need
their own folder (portable copies, see FILES) and their own port (page
Link, Port), each pointing at the other: 127.0.0.1:<its port>.


VIRTUAL-ON CARD
---------------

Virtual-On saves the player's progress on a card, as the cabinet did (the
Japanese cabinet only, Odin's default). FILE > New card... makes one;
F6 puts it in when the game asks, and takes it back at the end.


FILES
-----

Odin keeps its settings (odin.ini), the saves of each game, the states,
the artwork and its log (odin.log) in its folder:

 Linux    ~/.local/share/odin
 Windows  %APPDATA%\odin
 macOS    ~/Library/Application Support/odin

Each folder can be changed on the page Paths of the menu (games, BIOS,
artwork, saves, states, log). Another settings file: odin -c <file>.

Portable copy: put an empty file portable.txt beside the program; Odin
then keeps everything in that folder.
